msgctxt "help/1307/0"
msgid "The Accidental Emperor"
msgstr "Imperator durch Zufall"

msgctxt "help/1307/1"
msgid ""
"Caligula's reign had been so disastrous that the Senate made a brief "
"attempt to restore the Republic after the mad Emperor's assassination. "
"(Naturally, he was not made a god.) The Praetorian Guard preferred the "
"Principate, however, and by that point their wishes counted more than "
"those of the Senate. But who should hold the position? Choosing someone "
"of Caligula's line was out of the question, yet the soldiers wanted an "
"Emperor with the heritage of Julius Caesar and Augustus to lead them. "
"Tradition says that the Praetorian Guard roamed through the Imperial "
"Palace, looking for someone suitable, and eventually found Caligula's "
"uncle Claudius cowering behind a curtain. Caligula had had many of his "
"close relatives executed on trumped-up treason charges, a fate Claudius "
"had avoided by pretending to be a halfwit. The soldiers dragged the "
"startled and \n"
"#1306Previous Page# / #1308 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""
"Caligulas Herrschaft war so schlimm gewesen, daß der Senat einen kurzen "
"Versuch unternahm, nach der Hinrichtung des geistesgestörten Imperators "
"die Republik wieder einzuführen. (Natürlich wurde er nicht zum Gott "
"ernannt.) Die Prätorianer-Garde zog jedoch das Prinzipat vor, und zu "
"jenem Zeitpunkt zählten deren Wünsche mehr als die des Senats. Aber wer "
"sollte diese Position einnehmen? Jemanden aus Caligulas Verwandtschaft "
"zu nehmen, stand außer Diskussion, doch die Soldaten wollten einen "
"Imperator aus der Erbfolge von Julius Cäsar und Augustus zum Führer. "
"Ueberlieferungen berichten, daß die Prätorianer-Garde durch den "
"kaiserlichen Palast zog und nach jemanden passenden suchte. Schließlich "
"fanden sie Caligulas Onkel hinter einem Vorhang versteckt. Caligula "
"ließ viele seiner engen Verwandten nach erfundenen Anklagen des Verrats "
"hinrichten,\n"
"#1306Vorige Seite# / #1308 Nächste Seite#\n"
"#3Zurück zum Inhaltsverzeichnis#"

msgctxt "help/1308/0"
msgid "The Accidental Emperor (Cont.)"
msgstr "Imperator durch Zufall (Forts.)"

msgctxt "help/1308/1"
msgid ""
"protesting Claudius out of his hiding place and proclaimed him Emperor.\n"
"\n"
"Claudius's reign is somewhat difficult to analyze. Ancient historians "
"disliked him, and their accounts tend to be biased against the "
"accidental emperor. Other more objective historical evidence paints a "
"more positive picture of his rule. In any case, there is no question "
"that he was a vast improvement over his predecessor.\n"
"\n"
"Claudius was born into a family with many imperial connections and "
"famous names. His father, Nero Claudius Drusus, was brother to Tiberius "
"and a popular and successful general. Claudius himself was shy, "
"bookish, and physically unimpressive; the imperial family considered "
"him something of an \n"
"#1307Previous Page# / #1309 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""
"ein Schicksal, dem Claudius entging, indem er Schwachsinn vortäuschte. "
"Die Soldaten zerrten den verschreckten und protestierenden Claudius aus "
"seinem Versteck und erklärten ihn zum Imperator.\n"
"Claudius' Herrschaft ist schwer zu analysieren. Historiker der Antike "
"mochten ihn nicht, und ihre Aufzeichnungen neigten zu Vorurteilen gegen "
"den \"zufälligen\" Imperator. Andere, objektivere historische Quellen "
"zeichnen ein positiveres Bild seiner Regierungszeit. Jedenfalls steht "
"außer Frage, daß er im Vergleich zu seinem Vorgänger eine gewaltige "
"Verbesserung darstellte. \n"
"\n"
"Claudius wuchs in einer Familie auf, in der viele kaiserliche "
"Verbindungen und berühmte Namen zu finden waren. Sein Vater\n"
"\n"
"#1307Vorige Seite# / #1309 Nächste Seite#\n"
"#3Zurück zum Inhaltsverzeichnis#"

msgctxt "help/1309/0"
msgid "The Accidental Emperor (Cont.)"
msgstr "Imperator durch Zufall (Forts.)"

msgctxt "help/1309/1"
msgid ""
"embarrassment. Despite his later reputation as feeble-minded, he was in "
"truth an intelligent man, a student of Livy, and the author of several "
"dozen volumes of history on Rome and Etruria (as well as a few books on "
"dice games, his favorite pastime). His placement on the Imperial seat "
"was a surprise to everyone, including Claudius. As it turned out, the "
"soldiers could have made many worse choices than the quiet, deceptively "
"clever Claudius.\n"
"\n"
"The Senate at first were not particularly happy with the notion of "
"Emperor Claudius. There was an abortive attempt to bring back the "
"Republic, and several minor rebellions. A number of assassination "
"attempts were made on Claudius's life in his first year as Princeps. "
"But Claudius had the backing of the legions and was firm, even ruthless "
"sometimes, in \n"
"#1308Previous Page# / #1310 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""
"Nero Claudius Drusus war der Bruder von Tiberius und ein beliebter und "
"erfolgreicher General.Claudius selbst war scheu, belesen und körperlich "
"nicht sonderlich beeindruckend; der kaiserlichen Familie gefiel er ganz "
"und garnicht. Entgegen seinem späteren Ruf, dümmlich gewesen zu sein, "
"war er in Wahrheit ein intelligenter Mann. Er studierte bei Livius und "
"schrieb mehrere Dutzend Bände über die Geschichte von Rom und Etrurien "
"(ebenso einige Bücher über Würfelspiele, seine liebste "
"Freizeitbeschäftigung). Daß er auf den Kaiserthron kam, überraschte "
"jeden, am meisten ihn selbst. Wie sich herausstellte, hätten die "
"Soldaten eine schlechtere Wahl treffen können als den ruhigen, "
"unerwartet klugen Claudius.\n"
"\n"
"Der Senat war zunächst nicht sehr glücklich über den Imperator "
"Claudius. Es gab einen mißglückten\n"
"#1308Vorige Seite# / #1310 Nächste Seite#\n"
"#3Zurück zum Inhaltsverzeichnis#"

msgctxt "help/1310/0"
msgid "The Accidental Emperor (Cont.)"
msgstr "Imperator durch Zufall (Forts.)"

msgctxt "help/1310/1"
msgid ""
"eliminating resistance to his rule; fairly quickly the Senate settled "
"down and accepted him, though they never really liked him much. "
"Claudius had many friends in the freedman class, and made room for a "
"number of them in the civil service, an action the Senate found "
"distressingly low-bred.\n"
"\n"
"Claudius handled the external affairs of the Empire capably, if not "
"brilliantly. No soldier himself, he let others do his fighting for him. "
"Despite this, he was interested in expanding Rome's sphere of "
"influence, and made good progress in that direction: he added the North "
"African provinces of Caesariensis and Tingitana, created the province "
"of Lycia in Asia Minor, and annexed Thrace. From the point of view of "
"Western historians, his most noteworthy acquisition was the province "
"Britannia, where he \n"
"#1309Previous Page# / #1311 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""
"Versuch, die Republik wieder aufleben zu lassen, und mehrere kleine "
"Rebellionen. In seinem ersten Jahr als Prinzeps wurden einige Attentate "
"auf Claudius verübt, aber er hatte die Rückendeckung der Legionen und "
"schlug Widerstand gegen seine Herrschaft bestimmt und manchmal "
"rücksichtslos nieder. Bald beruhigte sich der Senat und akzeptierte "
"ihn, obwohl er ihn nie mochte. Claudius hatte viele Freunde in der "
"Klasse der freigelassenen Sklaven, von denen er auch einige im "
"Staatsdienst unterbrachte, was die Senatoren schrecklich ordinär fanden.\n"
"\n"
"Die auswärtigen Angelegenheiten des Imperiums meisterte Claudius "
"kompetent, wenn nicht gar brillant. Da er selbst kein Soldat war, ließ "
"er andere für sich kämpfen. Dennoch war er daran interessiert, Roms "
"Einflußsphäre auszuweiten, was ihm auch gelang. Er gewann die\n"
"#1309Vorige Seite# / #1311 Nächste Seite#\n"
"#3Zurück zum Inhaltsverzeichnis#"

msgctxt "help/1311/0"
msgid "The Accidental Emperor (Cont.)"
msgstr "Imperator durch Zufall (Forts.)"

msgctxt "help/1311/1"
msgid ""
"created the colonies of Camulodonum (Colchester) and Londinium "
"(London). He did not get carried away in his expansionism, however, "
"sensibly avoiding embroiling Rome in major wars with the Germanic "
"tribes or the Parthians.\n"
"\n"
"Claudius's civil administration was as efficient and moderate as his "
"foreign policy. Although he showed occasional flashes of caprice when "
"dealing with individuals, overall he improved the justice system, "
"extended the rights of citizenship to new groups, and was tolerant of "
"non-Roman customs and religions. He repaired the city and constructed "
"new public buildings, rebuilt and extended roads throughout the empire, "
"reorganized Rome's grain supply, modernized the harbor at Ostia, and "
"founded numerous Roman colonies.\n"
"\n"
"#1310Previous Page# / #1312 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""
"nordafrikanischen Provinzen Cäsariensis und Tingitana hinzu, schuf die "
"Provinz Lysien in Kleinasien und annektierte Thrakien. Aus der Sicht "
"westlicher Historiker war die erwähnenswerteste Errungenschaft die "
"Provinz Britannien, wo er die Kolonien Camulodonum (Colchester) und "
"Londinium (London) schuf. Dennoch hielt sich sein Expansionsdrang in "
"Grenzen; so vermied er vernünftigerweise die Verwicklung in einen Krieg "
"mit germanischen Stämmen oder den Parthern.\n"
"Claudius' zivile Verwaltung war ebenso tüchtig und gemäßigt wie seine "
"Außenpolitik. Auch wenn er ab und an launisch im Umgang mit Einzelnen "
"war, verbesserte er das allgemeine Gerichtswesen, weitete die "
"Bürgerrechte auf neue\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#1310Vorige Seite# / #1312 Nächste Seite#\n"
"#3Zurück zum Inhaltsverzeichnis#"

msgctxt "help/1312/0"
msgid "The Accidental Emperor (Cont.)"
msgstr "Imperator durch Zufall (Forts.)"

msgctxt "help/1312/1"
msgid ""
"Although his public affairs were well-ordered, Claudius continued the "
"Julio-Claudian tradition of having a messy private life. He married a "
"total of four times. He had his third wife executed for adultery, and "
"then amended Roman marriage laws to allow him to wed his own niece, "
"Agrippina the Younger, great-granddaughter of Augustus. This was "
"probably Agrippina's idea, not the Emperor's; Claudius's fourth wife "
"was a woman of ruthless ambition. She soon convinced Claudius to pass "
"over Brittanicus, his own son from an earlier marriage, and name her "
"son Lucius Domitius Ahenobarbus (son of her first husband Gnaeus "
"Domitius Ahenobarbus) as his heir. Once that was accomplished, "
"Agrippina promptly disposed of Claudius by feeding him poisonous "
"mushrooms. He died in 54 AD, after a thirteen year reign. \n"
"\n"
"#1311Previous Page# / #1313 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""
"Gruppen aus und war tolerant gegenüber nichtrömischen Bräuchen und "
"Religionen. Er sanierte die Stadt und baute neue öffentliche Gebäude, "
"reparierte und erweiterte das Straßennetz im Imperium, reorganisierte "
"Roms Getreideversorgung, modernisierte den Hafen von Ostia und gründete "
"zahlreiche römische Kolonien.\n"
"\n"
"Obwohl seine öffentlichen Geschäfte sehr geordnet verliefen, führte "
"Claudius die Julio-Claudianische Tradition eines verpfuschten "
"Privatlebens fort. Er heiratete insgesamt viermal. Seine dritte Frau "
"ließ er wegen Ehebruchs hinrichten und änderte dann das römische "
"Heiratsgesetz, damit er seine eigene Nichte heiraten konnte, Agrippina "
"die Jüngere, Großenkelin des Augustus, was vermutlich Agrippinas Idee "
"war und nicht die des Imperators: Claudius' vierte Frau besaß einen "
"rücksichtslosen Ehrgeiz.\n"
"#1311Vorige Seite# / #1450 Nächste Seite#\n"
"#3Zurück zum Inhaltsverzeichnis#"

msgctxt "help/1313/0"
msgid "The Accidental Emperor (Cont.)"
msgstr "Imperator durch Zufall (Forts.)"

msgctxt "help/1313/1"
msgid ""
"Ancient Roman historians tended to dismiss Claudius as a weak-willed "
"man who listened too much to his wives and his lowbrow freedmen "
"advisors. Of course, Roman historians were all upper class citizens, so "
"their opinion of Claudius couldn't help but be colored by his lack of "
"respect for their upper class privileges. Later historians have viewed "
"Claudius in a much gentler and more favorable light. Although not one "
"of the Roman Empire's most brilliant leaders, he was the only Emperor "
"of the Julio-Claudian line to come anywhere close to Augustus in "
"ability. After his death, the Senate laid their seal of approval on his "
"reign by granting him godhood.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#1312Previous Page# / #1314 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""
"Spätere Historiker haben Claudius wesentlich freundlicher und in "
"besserem Licht dargestellt. Auch wenn er nicht einer von Roms "
"brillantesten Führern war, so war er doch der einzige Imperator in der "
"Julio-Claudinischen Linie, der an Augustus' Fähigkeiten herankam. Nach "
"seinem Tod erkannten die Senatoren seine Leistungen dadurch an, indem "
"sie ihn in die Reihen der Götter aufnahmen.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#1450Vorige Seite# / #1314 Nächste Seite#\n"
"#3Zurück zum Inhaltsverzeichnis#"

